course: physics
lesson: 28
title: Electricity and safety
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Electricity flows wherever it finds a path to somewhere with a different push, such as the ground, and a human body can be that path.
explain: Ordinary water, the water in the bath, in puddles and in sweat, has salts dissolved in it that let electricity through, so wet hands and electrical things must never meet.
explain: A bird can sit happily on a power line because it touches only one wire and nothing else, so the electricity has no reason to go through it; a person on a ladder touching a wire and the ground at once gives it a path straight through the body.
explain: In a thunderstorm a car is a safe place: lightning runs over the outside of the metal body and into the ground, and it is the metal shell, not the rubber tyres, that protects you.
explain: Your own body runs on tiny electric signals too, far too weak to feel, which is why a strong shock from outside can upset your heart.
fact: Ordinary water carries electricity because of the salts and minerals dissolved in it, and so does a wet body.
fact: It is a myth that only pure water is the danger: very pure water carries electricity poorly, but tap water, bath water and sweat carry it well.
fact: Bathrooms have no ordinary sockets, and hair dryers must never be used near a bath.
fact: Sockets have little shutters inside the holes that stop things being pushed into the live parts; only a plug opens them properly.
fact: The electricity at a socket is strong enough to stop a heart.
fact: A bird on one power line is not hurt because there is no path through it to anywhere with a different push, such as the ground.
fact: In a thunderstorm, the metal body of a car carries lightning round the people inside and into the ground; the tyres are not what protects them.
fact: Lightning often hits the tallest thing around, and it can jump from a lone tree to a person standing under it; in a storm, go indoors.
fact: An electric eel has special cells that work like hundreds of tiny batteries in a row, and a large eel can knock a person over.
fact: A kite string, especially a wet one, can carry electricity from a power line to the person holding it.
fact: Nerves carry messages as tiny electric pulses, and doctors measure the heart's signals with stickers on the chest.
fact: The thick earth pin on a plug gives stray electricity a safe path into the ground, so metal kettles and washing machines are earthed.
exercise: Why must water and electricity never mix? | Ordinary water carries electricity, and so does a wet body | Water makes electricity explode like a firework in the air | Water turns electricity into steam at once | Electricity makes water poisonous to drink
exercise: What lets ordinary tap water carry electricity? | The salts and minerals dissolved in it | Its very cold temperature from the pipes | The tiny air bubbles that are trapped inside it | The chlorine that gives it its swimming-pool smell
exercise: Why are there no ordinary sockets in a bathroom? | Wet hands and water near sockets could give a shock | Bathroom walls are too thin for sockets | The steam in the air would slowly use up all the electricity | Sockets would make the bath water cold
exercise: What are the little shutters inside socket holes for? | To stop things being pushed into the live parts | To keep dust out so the plug stays shiny | To make the electricity come out slower | To stop the electricity leaking out of the wall at night
exercise: Why is a bird on a power line not hurt? | It touches only one wire, so there is no path through it | Birds' feathers soak up the electricity | Power lines are switched off for a moment when birds land | Birds are too small to feel electricity
exercise: Why would a person on a ladder touching a power line be in danger? | Touching the wire and the ground at once gives the electricity a path | Ladders pull electricity out of wires | People are much heavier than birds | The wire is cold enough to freeze the hand
exercise: What protects you inside a car in a thunderstorm? | The metal body carries the lightning round you to the ground | The thick rubber tyres stop the lightning from ever reaching you | The windows bounce the lightning away | The car radio absorbs the electricity
exercise: Why should you not shelter under a lone tree in a thunderstorm? | Lightning often hits the tallest thing and can jump from the tree to you | The rain under a tree is much colder | Trees attract lightning only when people stand there | The tree may suddenly start to grow
exercise: In a thunderstorm, what is the safest thing to do? | Go indoors | Stand under the tallest tree | Go for a swim in the lake | Hold a metal umbrella up high
exercise: How does an electric eel stun a fish? | Special cells in its body work like hundreds of tiny batteries in a row | It swallows small batteries that it finds on the riverbed | It rubs its skin on rocks to make static | It catches lightning when storms hit the river
exercise: What should you do if your kite gets caught on a power line? | Leave it, and tell an adult to call the power company | Pull hard on the string to free it | Climb the pole to unhook it | Throw a long stick up at it to knock it down off the line
exercise: How does your brain send messages to your fingers? | Nerves carry them as tiny electric pulses | Blood carries them as little sound waves | Muscles shout them down your arm | Bones pass them along like a telephone wire
exercise: What is the thick earth pin on a plug for? | It gives stray electricity a safe path into the ground | It makes the plug stronger so that it does not snap in the socket | It stores spare electricity for later | It makes the appliance work twice as fast
exercise: Why must you dry your hands before touching a switch or plug? | Water on your skin can let electricity flow through you | Wet hands make the metal in the switch rust instantly and stick | Dry hands push the switch harder | Water makes the lights flicker
exercise: How do doctors check the electric signals of your heart? | With stickers on your chest connected to a machine | By listening for crackles with a little microphone | By counting the tiny sparks that jump off your skin | By shining a bright torch through your ribs to look
test: "Pure water is what makes electricity deadly," says a quiz show. Put it right. | Everyday water with salts in it carries electricity; very pure water hardly does | Right, only pure water from a lab is ever dangerous | Right, salty water blocks electricity completely | Wrong, no kind of water carries electricity
test: mate says its fine in a storm in the car cos of the rubber tyres. true? | Safe, yes, but because of the metal body, not the tyres | Yes, rubber tyres stop any lightning | No, a car is the worst place in a storm | No, cars attract lightning straight inside to the people
test: Your little cousin is trying to push a hair clip into a socket. What should happen? | Stop her at once; a socket can give a deadly shock | Let her, the shutters make it totally safe for children | Let her, hair clips are too small to matter | Turn off the lights first, then let her
test: In a cartoon, a character dries her hair in the bath and nothing happens. What's wrong? | Water carries electricity, so it could kill her | Nothing, because the bath water blocks electricity | Hair dryers only ever blow out cold air near water | The bath water would just get pleasantly warm, that's all
test: Why can a bird sit on a wire, but a squirrel touching the wire and the pole at the same time gets a shock? | The squirrel makes a path to somewhere with a different push | Squirrels are made of metal inside | Birds have rubber feet | The pole sucks electricity out of the wire
test: shut up about the storm, I'm going out to fly my kite. good idea? | No, stay inside; lightning and kites are a deadly mix | Yes, storms give the best wind | Yes, if the string is dry | No, but only because it might rain and spoil the nice kite
test: Ali reckons electricity in your body is a myth. Is he right? | No, nerves and the heart use tiny electric signals | Yes, only robots and machines can ever use electricity | Yes, the human body runs only on chemicals and food | No, our bodies are charged up like batteries all day
test: What would happen if a metal kettle had a fault but no earth wire? | Touching it could send the electricity through you | The kettle would boil water faster | Nothing at all, because earth wires do nothing anyway | The water would turn into ice
test: Granddad is out in a field when thunder starts and there is no building nearby. What is best? | Stay low and away from tall things and open water | Run to the only tree in the field and shelter there | Lie down flat across the metal gate to keep off the grass | Stand right on top of the hill to watch it coming in
test: Why might a large electric eel be dangerous to a person wading in a river? | Its shock can be strong enough to knock a person over | It sucks the electricity right out of the person's body | It bites with electric teeth | It makes the water boil around you
test: Mia's kite string touched a power line, and it's raining. Why is that worse? | A wet string carries electricity even more easily to the person | Rain makes the power line switch off | Rain turns the string into rubber | A wet kite floats away from the line
exam: Identify the principal reason for which no ordinary socket outlets are installed within bathrooms. | Water present in such rooms readily conducts electricity through the body | The humidity therein would exhaust the electrical supply | Socket outlets are incompatible with tiled surfaces | Electrical current would render the bath water toxic
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the safety of a person within a motor vehicle during a thunderstorm? | The metallic body conducts any lightning around the occupants to earth | The rubber tyres insulate the vehicle against all lightning | The glass windows deflect lightning away from the vehicle | The vehicle affords no protection whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a bird perched upon a single conductor sustains no injury. | No path exists through its body to a point of differing potential | Its plumage absorbs the entirety of the current flowing in the line | The current is suspended whenever a bird alights | Birds are wholly immune to electric shock
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the earth pin of a plug. | It affords stray current a safe path into the ground | It augments the power delivered to the appliance | It retains surplus electricity for later use | It serves solely to strengthen the plug mechanically
